      <p><strong>Increases in Successful Remediation</strong></p>
      <p>All five states with performance accountability systems improved their rates of remedial success in some flash- ion, but the measures used varied considerably. For example:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>In Florida, over a six-year period, the percent of remedial students passing the highest remedial course within two years increased by 7.6 percentage points for reading, 4.3 percentage points for math, and 4.6 percentage points for writing.</li>
        <li>In Washington, over a four-year period, the percent age of ESL, ABE, and GED students who gained one competency level in at least one subject area during a year increased by 14 percentage points.</li>
        <li>In Illinois, over a three-year period, remedial credits earned as a percentage of remedial credits attempted increased by 1 percentage point. However, in California—a state without performance funding— the same measure showed a 3 percentage points increase over a five-year period.</li>
      </ul>
